  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">India Partnership for Early Learning (IPEL)</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">(India):  ..Using FY 2022 funds, USAID/India will continue to support the India Partnership for Early Learning activity focusing on Foundational Literacy and Numeracy (FLN). The activity will support the state governments and institutions by increasing their capacity to leverage and coordinate financial, human, and material resources to implement evidence-based service delivery in FLN. The ultimate objective is to steadily increase the percentage of students attaining age and grade appropriate proficiencies in the reading, math, cognitive, and social and emotional domains...The activity will build state/national government capability in designing and developing reporting systems, curriculum, classroom pedagogy, teacher training for &quot;reading&quot; instruction, and the use of education technology where appropriate. By identifying, institutionalizing, and scaling best practices, as well as investing in systemic processes such as teacher training and curriculum development the activity will not only be able to increase reading levels of an increased number of learners it will also reduce barriers to literacy  and improve access to girls and women. These efforts together will ensure more learners who read at grade level. ..USAID/India will leverage innovative partnerships and financing strategies by engaging with the private sector and focusing on the development of innovative financing models to leverage private investment, Corporate Social Responsibility funds, grant capital, and philanthropic funds. The aim will be to accelerate enterprise-driven solutions to assist states in martialing successful managerial, operational, and technical strategies across the education value chain involving both public and private actors so that they can deliver high-quality foundational skills instruction to all students under their care, regardless of the type of primary or pre-primary school they attend..</narrative>
  </description>
